# Keeps the build context to what a fresh CI checkout contains, so a LOCAL
# `docker build` is a faithful rehearsal of the platform build.
#
# The platform recipe's Dockerfile installs dependencies from composer.json/lock
# FIRST (for layer caching) and only then does `COPY . /tmp/magento/`. Everything
# below is either absent in CI or regenerated by the build, so ignoring it changes
# nothing about the image — it only stops a developer's working copy leaking in.
#
# Three entries are load-bearing rather than cosmetic:
#
#   app/etc/env.php — gitignored, so CI never has one, but a developer's points at
#     their local MySQL on 127.0.0.1. Copied into the build it makes the DB-LESS
#     `setup:di:compile` try to reach that database and die with a bare
#     "SQLSTATE[HY000] [2002] Connection refused" — which reads like a broken
#     Dockerfile rather than a stray file, and costs real time to trace. The runtime
#     stage installs .platform/env.php over it anyway.
#
#   vendor/ + generated/ + pub/static/ + pub/media/ — a local build would copy the
#     developer's own versions OVER the ones composer just installed, so the image
#     would ship whatever happened to be on that laptop. pub/media matters here in
#     particular: `mage-os/sample-data-media` is installed INTO pub/media by composer
#     inside the builder, and an ignored host pub/media is what keeps it intact.
#
#   .git — without this the whole repository history is baked into the published
#     image (size, and anything ever committed and later removed).
#
# `auth.json` is deliberately NOT ignored: the builder stage needs it for composer
# (the recipe Dockerfile COPYs it unconditionally), and it is deleted inside that
# stage so it never reaches the runtime image. It cannot be excluded here without
# breaking the build.
#
# app/etc/config.php is deliberately NOT ignored: it is the committed module
# contract and MUST reach the build.
.git
.github
.idea
app/etc/env.php
vendor/
generated/
node_modules/
var/
pub/static/
pub/media/
docker-compose.yml
